# ant serial capture v1: <seconds|-> <dir> <framed message, hex>
# HAND-ASSEMBLED, NOT A CAPTURE. Nothing here came off a wire. Every frame was
# built from the message tables in docs/ant-serial-protocol.md and the XOR was
# worked term by term in the comment above it. Do not cite as an observation.
# See tools/vectors/README.md. Replayed by tools/test_ant_golden.py.
#
# The timestamp column is '-' throughout, the same form tools/ant_conformance.py
# writes, because nothing here is a statement about timing.

# case reset
# MESG_SYSTEM_RESET, [channel 0]. Every host library opens with this.
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 4A = EF   EF ^ 00 = EF
- > a4014a00ef
# MESG_STARTUP_MESG, [STARTUP_COMMAND_RESET 0x20]. 0x4A is answered by 0x6F and
# by no 0x40 at all - the one exception to command/response.
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 6F = CA   CA ^ 20 = EA
- < a4016f20ea

# case capabilities
# MESG_REQUEST, [index 0, wanted id MESG_CAPABILITIES 0x54].
#   A4 ^ 02 = A6   A6 ^ 4D = EB   EB ^ 00 = EB   EB ^ 54 = BF
- > a4024d0054bf
# MESG_CAPABILITIES, nine bytes. This payload is the one thing in this file
# that is real: it is what the firmware answers, recorded in
# docs/ant-serial-protocol.md and as ant_wire.OBSERVED_CAPABILITIES.
#   A4^09=AD  ^54=F9  ^08=F1  ^08=F9  ^00=F9  ^B2=4B
#   ^32=79    ^00=79  ^FD=84  ^8D=09  ^0F=06
- < a40954080800b23200fd8d0f06

# case network-key
# MESG_NETWORK_KEY, [network 0, B9 A5 21 FB BD 72 C3 45]. The ANT+ key is
# public and is already in tools/ant_scan.py; it is a network key, not a
# protocol constant, which is why it stays out of the generator.
#   A4^09=AD  ^46=EB  ^00=EB  ^B9=52  ^A5=F7  ^21=D6
#   ^FB=2D    ^BD=90  ^72=E2  ^C3=21  ^45=64
- > a4094600b9a521fbbd72c34564
# MESG_RESPONSE_EVENT, [channel 0, 0x46, RESPONSE_NO_ERROR].
#   A4^03=A7  ^40=E7  ^00=E7  ^46=A1  ^00=A1
- < a40340004600a1

# case assign-channel
# MESG_ASSIGN_CHANNEL, [channel 1, CHANNEL_TYPE_SLAVE_RX_ONLY 0x40, network 0].
# Chosen because its checksum comes out 0xA4: the frame ends in a byte that
# looks like the start of the next one. Leave SYNC out of the sum and you get
# 0x00 instead, which looks entirely unremarkable.
#   A4^03=A7  ^42=E5  ^01=E4  ^40=A4  ^00=A4
- > a40342014000a4
# MESG_RESPONSE_EVENT, [channel 1, 0x42, RESPONSE_NO_ERROR]. Its checksum is
# 0xA4 as well, so this pair puts two false SYNC bytes back to back.
#   A4^03=A7  ^40=E7  ^01=E6  ^42=A4  ^00=A4
- < a40340014200a4

# case lib-config
# MESG_ANTLIB_CONFIG, [filler 0, LIB_CONFIG_ALL_EXT_FIELDS 0xE0] - channel id
# and RSSI and RX timestamp, which is what ant_verify.py asks for.
#   A4^02=A6  ^6E=C8  ^00=C8  ^E0=28
- > a4026e00e028
# MESG_RESPONSE_EVENT, [channel 0, 0x6E, RESPONSE_NO_ERROR].
#   A4^03=A7  ^40=E7  ^00=E7  ^6E=89  ^00=89
- < a40340006e0089

# case open-channel
# MESG_OPEN_CHANNEL, [channel 1].
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 4B = EE   EE ^ 01 = EF
- > a4014b01ef
# MESG_RESPONSE_EVENT, [channel 1, 0x4B, RESPONSE_NO_ERROR].
#   A4^03=A7  ^40=E7  ^01=E6  ^4B=AD  ^00=AD
- < a40340014b00ad

# case broadcast
# MESG_BROADCAST_DATA, [channel 1, eight 0xA4 data bytes]. There is no escaping
# in this protocol, so a payload byte equal to SYNC is legal and a reader must
# be driven by LEN. Eight identical bytes XOR to zero, so the sum is short:
#   A4^09=AD  ^4E=E3  ^01=E2  ^(A4 eight times)=E2
- < a4094e01a4a4a4a4a4a4a4a4e2

# The three cases below declare nonconformance, which tools/test_ant_golden.py
# reads from the token 'malformed' in the case name: a problem with a record is
# a failure unless its case declares it, in which case it is recorded and
# waived. They cover the three shapes the waiver has to serve, and between them
# they are what keeps both branches of that rule exercised in CI.

# case malformed/sync-omitted-checksum
# FRAMING-LEVEL. The historical bug, on the wire. MESG_REQUEST with the
# checksum computed from LEN onwards instead of from SYNC:
# 0x02 ^ 0x4D ^ 0x00 ^ 0x54 = 0x1B, where the correct value is 0xBF. Note
# 0xBF ^ 0x1B = 0xA4 - the error is always exactly the SYNC byte. unframe()
# must throw this away.
- > a4024d00541b

# case malformed/short-payload
# SEMANTIC. MESG_ASSIGN_CHANNEL declares 3..4 payload bytes; this carries 1.
# The frame itself is impeccable - SYNC, LEN and checksum all agree - and that
# is exactly what makes it worth sending: it tests the bridge's error mapping,
# not its framer. This is the shape ant_conformance.py calls malformed-short,
# and the shape the first draft of the harness wrongly demanded be rejected.
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ 42 = E7   E7 ^ 01 = E6
- > a4014201e6
# The refusal: MESG_RESPONSE_EVENT [channel 1, 0x42, INVALID_MESSAGE 0x28].
# Fully conformant, and it sits inside a malformed case on purpose - a waiver
# is only spent on a record that actually has a problem, so this one is still
# checked like any other.
#   A4^03=A7  ^40=E7  ^01=E6  ^42=A4  ^28=8C
- < a403400142288c

# case malformed/unknown-id
# UNKNOWN ID. Message id 0xFE is in no table. In a capture from a real host
# that is a hard failure and information to absorb into
# protocol/ant_wire.yaml; here the case name says it is deliberate. A
# Device0.txt capture carries no case names at all, which is what keeps the two
# apart with no extra vocabulary.
#   A4 ^ 01 = A5   A5 ^ FE = 5B   5B ^ 00 = 5B
- > a401fe005b
